    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on some deck plans for a client in vancouver. it's a cedar deck attached to a house. i need to make sure my math is right for the load specs before i submit for permits. can you explain the general difference between how i should calculate live load vs dead load for the beam and post spacing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For deck structure calculations, the dead load (including the cedar materials and joists) is calculated by multiplying the spacing between joists by the thickness of the wood to find pounds per square foot, which you then multiply by the tributary area of each beam. The live load, typically 40 psf for residential decks in Vancouver per the BC Building Code, covers the walking, furniture, and debris scenarios you must also assign per square foot. When determining reaction forces for your beams and posts, sum the total line loads from both calculations to ensure the members are sized correctly to support the total weight without exceeding design limits. You should verify these totals against the specific span distances of your piles and beams to ensure full compliance with permit requirements.",
