    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so if i've got a 16ft span for the main beam, what kind of error margin should i be looking at if the wood has slight inconsistencies or if there's unexpected weight like heavy planters or snow buildup? i want to make sure nothing unexpected happens to the structure over time.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Structural engineering relies heavily on significant safety factors rather than a simple percentage error margin to account for uncertainties like wood variability, heavy planters, and snow loads in Vancouver. While exact percentages vary by code, designers typically use safety factors around 1.67 for live loads and 2.5 or higher for dead loads to ensure the structure can handle these extra stresses without permanent deformation or failure. For residential deck beams, it is generally unacceptable to operate; instead, you must size the lumber so that the calculated load is significantly below the material's actual capacity for every possible span length. Always consult your specific structural engineer, who will apply the precise safety margins required for the BC Building Code's seismic and environmental loads.",
